# DURABLE REPRO -- a closure literal that declares a `&`-sigil parameter
# (`&x`) and whose body ASSIGNS through that parameter (`x = ...`, `x += ...`)
# fails to PARSE with "expected ')', found '='". The read-only twin
# (`(int &x): print(x)`) parses fine, so the gap is in the closure body
# parser: after the `:`, an `x = ...` at statement position is misparsed
# because the enclosing `(...)` is treated as expression-parenthesization
# and `=` at expression position is not a valid token.
#
# MEASURED 2026-07-27 at HEAD 1028972f (Track B3 scout):
#   gg check                       -> parse error at the `=`
#
# The read-only shape works:
#   ((int &x): print(x))(&a)       -> parses; SEGVs at runtime (Track B1 target
#                                     of the callable-value SEGV class -- an
#                                     independent lowering defect)
#
# IMPACT:
#   - blocks writing an IIFE that mutates its captured argument -- forces
#     naming the closure into a local (`Callable[void(&int)] cb = (int &x):
#     x = x + 100; cb(&a)`)
#   - blocked the Track B3 scout's IIFE cell probe (G2/G3): the scout could
#     not test the semantic sigil gate on IIFE callee shape because the
#     mismatched shapes did not reach `gg check` -- they died at parse.
#
# INTENDED (Option A, D31-uniform under B3): PARSE, then behave identically
# to the equivalent named-closure form:
#   1. `((int &x): x = x + 100)(&a)`  -> accept, run, print 101.
#   2. `((int &x): x = x + 100)(a)`   -> REJECT E_OwnershipMismatch (B3's
#                                        semantic gate; asserted by the sibling
#                                        NEG fixtures once this parses).
#
# Fix direction: extend `parse_closure` (or the closure-body parser) so a
# body starting with an identifier-followed-by-assignment forms a statement,
# not a mis-classified expression. The read-only case works because
# `print(x)` is a call expression -- the assignment case needs the same
# statement-position path a top-level function body has.
#
# Cited from TODO.md -- anchor: grep -nF -- 'IIFE parse' TODO.md
void main():
    int a = 1
    # THE DEFECT: this line fails at parse today; it should parse, run,
    # and print 101 under Option A (D31-uniform + parse fix).
    ((int &x): x = x + 100)(&a)
    print(a)
